[
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110049\">@Mario Carneiro</span> (or others who understand <code>lean4lean</code>), my AIs claim there are two errors in lean4lean, could you take a look at this?</p>",
        "id": 629219276,
        "sender_full_name": "Kim Morrison",
        "timestamp": 1791201977
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"robot\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f916\" role=\"img\" title=\"robot\">:robot:</span> says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">axiom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">abstract_eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Expr</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">xs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FVarId</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">e.abstract</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">xs.map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">fvar</span><span class=\"o\">⟩</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e.abstractList</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">xs</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>Expr.abstract</code> is implemented in C++, so <code>Verify/Axioms.lean</code> gives it a pure Lean reference implementation (<code>abstract1</code> for one fvar at depth <code>d</code>, <code>abstractList</code> folding <code>abstract1</code> over the list) and this axiom asserts the two agree. They do not: <code>abstract1</code> shifts loose bound variables (<code>.bvar i, d =&gt; .bvar (if i &lt; d then i else i + 1)</code>), while the C++ <code>abstract</code> only replaces fvars and leaves loose bvars alone:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"k\">#eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Expr.bvar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">abstract</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">fvar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⟨</span><span class=\"ss\">`x</span><span class=\"o\">⟩]</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Lean.Expr.bvar 0        (abstractList gives bvar 1)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">#eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Expr.app</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">bvar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">fvar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⟨</span><span class=\"ss\">`x</span><span class=\"o\">⟩))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">abstract</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">fvar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⟨</span><span class=\"ss\">`x</span><span class=\"o\">⟩,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">fvar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⟨</span><span class=\"ss\">`y</span><span class=\"o\">⟩]</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- app (bvar 3) (bvar 1)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>It is also false for duplicate lists: the C++ (<code>abstract.cpp</code>) searches from the last element and emits <code>offset + n - i - 1</code>, so the <em>last</em> occurrence wins, while the sequential <code>abstractList</code> gives the first occurrence the lower index. A true replacement is a one-pass <code>abstractN xs e k</code> (<code>xs[i] ↦ bvar (k + n - 1 - i)</code>, last occurrence wins, loose bvars unchanged) with the axiom <code>e.abstract ⟨xs.map .fvar⟩ = e.abstractN xs</code>; the old statement then follows exactly when <code>xs.Nodup</code> and <code>e.looseBVarRange = 0</code>, which is presumably every use through <code>LocalContext.mkBinding_eq</code>. </p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 629219336,
        "sender_full_name": "Kim Morrison",
        "timestamp": 1791201991
    },
